EXT. STOCKYARD AUCTION RING - DAY

Plank bleachers. Dust. A hand-lettered sign: LOT 14, HEIFER, OPEN BIDDING. Flies work the heat.

HOLLIS BRADDOCK, 60s, a hat older than his marriage, sits in the third row with his hands in his lap. Beside him MARIBEL BRADDOCK, 50s, stares straight ahead, ring finger bare. Across the aisle TAVISH DOYLE, 40s, in a clean vest, a bidder's card in his fist, doesn't look at either of them.

DOLLY, 12, Hollis's granddaughter, sits at the rail with her boots dangling and watches all three.

AUCTIONEER (O.S.)
(over the loudspeaker)
Who'll give me four? Four hundred, four, I got four, now five, five, who'll give five?

Tavish lifts his card. Hollis does not move. Maribel's jaw tightens.

AUCTIONEER (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Five, I got five from the man in the vest, now six? Six for a good milker, six?

Hollis shifts in his seat. Takes off his hat, turns it twice by the brim, puts it back on. Maribel slides one foot six inches away from his. Dolly picks at a splinter and keeps looking at her grandfather's hands.

AUCTIONEER (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Six anywhere? Going once --

Hollis lifts one finger. Barely.

AUCTIONEER (O.S.) (CONT'D)
Six, I got six! Seven?

Tavish glances over, nods at Hollis, once. Lifts his card.

MARIBEL
(to the air)
Eleven years.

HOLLIS
Mm.

AUCTIONEER (O.S.)
Seven, going --

Hollis lowers his hand. Dolly looks at Maribel. Maribel looks at Tavish.

Gavel.
